.TH find 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
find \- walk a directory tree and act on what is there
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B find
.I "directory ..."
[\fIexpression\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B find
descends each
.I directory
and evaluates
.I expression
against every file it meets.  If the expression contains no action, an
implied
.B \-print
is added, so
.B find .
lists everything below the current directory.
.SH PRIMARIES
.TP
.BI \-name " pattern"
The file name matches
.IR pattern ,
in which
.BR * ,
.B ?
and
.B [ ]
have their shell meanings.  Quote it against the shell.
.TP
.BI \-type " c"
Type
.IR c :
.B f
plain,
.B d
directory,
.B b
block device,
.B c
character device,
.B p
FIFO.
.TP
.BI \-perm " mode"
The permission bits are exactly
.IR mode ,
which is octal.  A leading minus means all of those bits are set,
whatever else is.
.TP
.BI \-links " n"
Has
.I n
links.
.TP
.BI \-user " name"
Owned by that user.
.TP
.BI \-group " name"
In that group.
.TP
.BI \-size " n"
Is
.I n
blocks of 512 bytes long.
.TP
.BI \-inum " n"
Has that inode number.
.TP
.BI \-atime " n"
Read
.I n
days ago.
.TP
.BI \-mtime " n"
Written
.I n
days ago.
.TP
.BI \-ctime " n"
Changed
.I n
days ago.
.TP
.BI \-newer " file"
Modified more recently than
.IR file .
.PP
Numbers may be written
.I n
for exactly,
.BI + n
for more than, or
.BI \- n
for less than.
.SH ACTIONS
.TP
.B \-print
Print the path.
.TP
.BI \-exec " command" " ;"
Run
.IR command .
An argument of
.B {}
is replaced by the current path, and the semicolon that ends the command
must be quoted from the shell.  True if the command exits 0.
.TP
.BI \-ok " command" " ;"
The same, but ask first.
.TP
.B \-nop
Do nothing, and be true.  Useful to stop the implied
.BR \-print .
.SH COMBINING
.B \-a
is and and may be left out,
.B \-o
is or,
.B !
negates, and parentheses group.  The shell needs the parentheses and
the
.B !
quoted.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# find / -name core -print
# find . -type f -mtime -1
# find /tmp -mtime +7 -exec rm {} ;
# find . -name *.bak -ok rm {} ;
.fi
.SH NOTES
There is no
.BR \-maxdepth ,
no
.BR \-delete ,
no
.B \-print0
and no
.BR \-regex .
.PP
Because
.BR rm (1)
ignores any argument starting with a dash,
.B "find ... \-exec rm {} ;"
is the way to remove a tree here.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ls (1),
.BR rm (1),
.BR xargs (1),
.BR du (1)
